reims-vgpu: the render Store converts the present's frame beside the guest publish.

A native-float render Store of the presented RGBA16F front surface (1206x2622,
25 MB of half floats) did three whole-frame things one after the other on the
drain, under the device lock: read the resident back
(native_float_readback_us), convert that readback into the BGRA8 frame the
present takes (store_capture::stash, store_capture_convert_us), and copy the
same readback into the guest's pages (native_float_publish_us). A recorded
live second on the development host (i9-13900K, RX 6800-class GPU) with 46
Stores: 69 ms, 86 ms and 105 ms, about 1.5, 1.9 and 2.3 ms a Store, out of a
306 ms chain_phase store_us. The conversion and the publish only read the
staging bytes, and neither needs anything the other produces.

Now the writer runs them side by side: PreparedNativeImageWrite gains
write_from_alongside, which hands the borrowed source bytes to a scoped thread
running the caller's reader while this thread copies them into the guest's
pages, and joins that thread before it drops the source, so the reader never
outlives the staging lease and gets no device state, host or engine handle.
write_from is the same call with no reader. store_capture::stash takes the
texel kind and the borrowed bytes (readback_bytes_into_bgra, the bytes-level
half of texels_into_bgra, so both callers still run one conversion). Seal,
take and every refusal are unchanged; a thread that cannot start runs the
reader first, as before (store route native_image_side_serial_n).

native_float_publish_us now spans the overlapped pair. Offline, the
store_overlap_timing test (FakeHost, 16 KiB pages scattered through guest RAM,
the vphone geometry; the host shared with a running phone): conversion then
publish 6.0-7.0 ms, side by side 3.8-4.4 ms (medians of 21). Not measured on a
live phone.

